WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.834 --> 00:00:03.400
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-nominated comedian

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.868
who will be returning as
guest host of "The Daily Show"

00:00:05.868 --> 00:00:07.767
next week on Comedy Central,
and her memoir,

00:00:07.767 --> 00:00:09.767
"Leslie [Bleep] Jones,"
is on sale now.

00:00:09.767 --> 00:00:11.834
Please welcome back to the show,
our good friend,

00:00:11.834 --> 00:00:13.767
Leslie [Bleep] Jones, everybody!

00:00:13.767 --> 00:00:16.767
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.767 --> 00:00:23.501
♪♪

00:00:23.501 --> 00:00:30.200
♪♪

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:36.934
♪♪

00:00:36.934 --> 00:00:43.634
♪♪

00:00:43.634 --> 00:00:46.000
Welcome back, my friend.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.200
[ Cheering ]

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.067
Yeah.

00:00:49.067 --> 00:00:50.234
[ Cheering ]

00:00:50.234 --> 00:00:52.734
If you got it, you got it.
Flaunt it.

00:00:52.734 --> 00:00:55.667
-My stylist said, "Make sure
that you show the leg, bitch."

00:00:55.667 --> 00:00:56.901
-Yeah.

00:00:56.901 --> 00:00:58.934
[ Cheering ]

00:00:58.934 --> 00:01:00.667
-I've been lucky enough
to talk to you

00:01:00.667 --> 00:01:02.067
about this book
a couple of times now.

00:01:02.067 --> 00:01:05.501
You did a wonderful book event
in New York City.

00:01:05.501 --> 00:01:07.133
You came on a podcast
with me and my brother

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:09.033
and can I say --
-You're so sweet.

00:01:09.033 --> 00:01:11.767
-Oh, I love you.
-Seth loves me!

00:01:11.767 --> 00:01:13.667
-I will say,
we did a book event.

00:01:13.667 --> 00:01:14.534
-We should have a baby.

00:01:14.534 --> 00:01:16.834
-What?
-Nothing.

00:01:16.834 --> 00:01:20.200
[ Laughter ]

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:21.434
-[ Sighs ]

00:01:21.434 --> 00:01:22.834
-Did you see how he reacted?

00:01:22.834 --> 00:01:24.434
Like we had really did
something.

00:01:24.434 --> 00:01:27.634
Like, "You pregnant?"

00:01:27.634 --> 00:01:29.067
-I mean,
the book event went well.

00:01:29.067 --> 00:01:31.767
I don't remember going
that well.

00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:33.167
I would say, it was

00:01:33.167 --> 00:01:35.300
a great symphony space
on the Upper West Side,

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:36.634
and I feel like
a lot of those people

00:01:36.634 --> 00:01:38.067
go to book events
on the Upper West Side,

00:01:38.067 --> 00:01:40.267
and I think it was
they've never heard answers

00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:42.868
about a book that you gave
at that book event.

00:01:42.868 --> 00:01:43.968
-I loved it so much.

00:01:43.968 --> 00:01:45.868
-Yeah?
-You had no control over me.

00:01:45.868 --> 00:01:49.300
-No, I almost never do.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:51.267
Now, did your editors have
control over you in your book?

00:01:51.267 --> 00:01:52.934
-No. Absolutely not.

00:01:52.934 --> 00:01:54.534
Absolutely not.
They kept asking for it.

00:01:54.534 --> 00:01:55.801
They were like,
"Are you finished?"

00:01:55.801 --> 00:01:58.067
No, I'm not finished.

00:01:58.067 --> 00:02:00.534
-Did they fight you on the title
or was the title...?

00:02:00.534 --> 00:02:02.434
-They fought me on everything.
-Yeah.

00:02:02.434 --> 00:02:04.167
-Look, okay, there's a deadline.

00:02:04.167 --> 00:02:06.767
There's a deadline and I --
yeah, blew the deadline.

00:02:06.767 --> 00:02:08.167
Yeah.

00:02:08.167 --> 00:02:11.234
I reread the whole book
and rewrote the whole book,

00:02:11.234 --> 00:02:15.100
and they was like,
"You can't do that."

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:19.167
It's my book. I can do whatever
the [bleep] I want to!

00:02:19.167 --> 00:02:21.334
[ Cheering ]
-I want to know --

00:02:21.334 --> 00:02:23.701
I've heard people --
You did something, though

00:02:23.701 --> 00:02:25.367
I have not heard
anyone say they've done before

00:02:25.367 --> 00:02:27.334
when you talk about,
you can't be controlled,

00:02:27.334 --> 00:02:28.968
even you couldn't control you,

00:02:28.968 --> 00:02:31.667
because you, when you went in
to do the audiobook,

00:02:31.667 --> 00:02:33.534
you admitted you wouldn't
even read it

00:02:33.534 --> 00:02:35.934
the way you wrote it.
-Okay.

00:02:35.934 --> 00:02:37.767
-It went just like this.

00:02:37.767 --> 00:02:39.434
It went just like this.

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:45.434
"Foreword.

00:02:45.434 --> 00:02:47.133
When Leslie --" No.

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:52.734
First, what had happened was --

00:02:52.734 --> 00:02:54.067
[ Laughter ]

00:02:54.067 --> 00:02:56.434
-And the editor was like,
"I made her take out

00:02:56.434 --> 00:02:57.734
all the
what had happened wases."

00:02:57.734 --> 00:03:00.434
-Yo, yo, the director was like,

00:03:00.434 --> 00:03:01.701
"Um, I don't know, maybe we --"

00:03:01.701 --> 00:03:04.400
I was like, "Girl,
this is how it's going, girl.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.601
I'm not finna sit here
and read this book and be like,

00:03:06.601 --> 00:03:09.868
'Oh, Leslie Jones,
she said that she --'

00:03:09.868 --> 00:03:11.567
But since a cop had --'"

00:03:11.567 --> 00:03:15.200
No, I know the story,

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:16.634
and I'm finna
give you the details.

00:03:16.634 --> 00:03:18.868
I'm going to give you
the real tea.

00:03:18.868 --> 00:03:21.067
They didn't put that in there.
They was trying to hide it.

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:22.400
Let me tell you.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:25.300
I think it's the rarest case
where, you know, like,

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:27.434
sometimes when I'll be --
I'll like, read a book

00:03:27.434 --> 00:03:30.968
on like, my Kindle and then
I'll switch over to Audible.

00:03:30.968 --> 00:03:32.767
When you go back, it's like,
"you were on this page."

00:03:32.767 --> 00:03:36.234
I feel like with your book,
it'd be like, "We don't know.

00:03:36.234 --> 00:03:37.834
We don't know where you are.
We don't know what to tell you."

00:03:37.834 --> 00:03:40.133
-I was like, "Just ride with me.
Tell me if I go too deep."

00:03:40.133 --> 00:03:42.133
And there was some stories
I would go so deep in it,

00:03:42.133 --> 00:03:44.567
she'd be like, "Um, Leslie,

00:03:44.567 --> 00:03:50.100
we're -- we're only in
Chapter 2 and we're over.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:51.534
It's over, that whole chapter."

00:03:51.534 --> 00:03:54.067
And I was like,
"No, I got more to say."

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:56.167
So I thought it was
only 7 hours.

00:03:56.167 --> 00:04:00.133
I come to find out
it's 16 hours long.

00:04:00.133 --> 00:04:03.834
[ Laughter ]

00:04:03.834 --> 00:04:08.367
-It's the "War and Peace"
of audio memoirs.

00:04:08.367 --> 00:04:11.667
Now, I want to get --
real talk here.

00:04:11.667 --> 00:04:13.567
You actually wrote this memoir,
and you had to go back

00:04:13.567 --> 00:04:15.000
and you had to remember things,

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.467
and you maybe had to revisit
tougher times in your life.

00:04:17.467 --> 00:04:20.167
Was it cathartic
to go back to those things?

00:04:20.167 --> 00:04:22.667
Was it difficult, ever,
to go back to the past?

00:04:22.667 --> 00:04:25.067
-Oh, of course,
because now you look and I go,

00:04:25.067 --> 00:04:26.501
"Oh, my God,
I made it through that?

00:04:26.501 --> 00:04:28.000
Oh, my God, how did I not die?

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.534
Oh, my God,
why would I say that?

00:04:30.534 --> 00:04:31.634
Oh, my God!"

00:04:31.634 --> 00:04:32.734
It was a lot of stuff in there,

00:04:32.734 --> 00:04:34.067
I was like,
"Girl, you can't say that.

00:04:34.067 --> 00:04:35.133
Oh, you have to take that out."

00:04:35.133 --> 00:04:36.133
And then there was
a lot of things --

00:04:36.133 --> 00:04:37.234
because, you know,
I smoke weed,

00:04:37.234 --> 00:04:38.434
so I don't remember
a lot of stuff

00:04:38.434 --> 00:04:40.200
and oh, my God.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:41.267
You know Dina, right?

00:04:41.267 --> 00:04:43.434
You know
her last name was Moles?

00:04:43.434 --> 00:04:44.567
-Uh-huh, Dina Moles, yeah.

00:04:44.567 --> 00:04:46.734
-I call her Morales.
Her new name is Morales.

00:04:46.734 --> 00:04:49.400
-Okay, so you called her
he wrong name in the book?

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:50.667
-There's so many things.

00:04:50.667 --> 00:04:54.501
So my coach hit me,
and he was like, "Yeah,

00:04:54.501 --> 00:04:56.167
that's that's not
exactly how I went."

00:04:56.167 --> 00:04:57.734
I was like,
"It's how I remembered it."

00:04:57.734 --> 00:04:59.334
That's how I started saying it.

00:04:59.334 --> 00:05:03.300
I was like, "Well,
this is the story I remember."

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:06.534
No, it's really pretty accurate.

00:05:06.534 --> 00:05:08.200
[ Laughter ]

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:10.767
-You talk about some people --

00:05:10.767 --> 00:05:12.734
Look, you came across, you know,

00:05:12.734 --> 00:05:14.968
you broke into "SNL"
later in your career

00:05:14.968 --> 00:05:16.534
than maybe other people,
but you met

00:05:16.534 --> 00:05:18.801
a lot of very successful
people over your path,

00:05:18.801 --> 00:05:20.300
and some of them
gave you a lot of advice.

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:23.267
Some told you about the value
of getting life experience

00:05:23.267 --> 00:05:25.300
as well, outside of
just getting onstage.

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:27.400
What were some of the things
you did that were helpful?

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:30.167
-Yeah, I just went
to a lot of jobs.

00:05:30.167 --> 00:05:33.400
I had a lot of jobs,
a lot of jobs.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:37.868
Yeah, I got fired a lot.
-Yeah.

00:05:37.868 --> 00:05:39.601
I -- yeah --

00:05:39.601 --> 00:05:40.667
Did I ever I quit?

00:05:40.667 --> 00:05:42.434
I quit, but yeah. No.

00:05:42.434 --> 00:05:43.834
-Did you do a lot of, like,

00:05:43.834 --> 00:05:45.501
"You can't fire me, I quit,"
because that doesn't count.

00:05:45.501 --> 00:05:47.634
-No, I was
never in that situation.

00:05:47.634 --> 00:05:49.567
It was always like,
"You can go."

00:05:49.567 --> 00:05:52.534
Like, "We're going
to let you go now."

00:05:52.534 --> 00:05:54.901
It was always like,
"Oh, we can't keep you on."

00:05:54.901 --> 00:05:56.934
"Uh,
going a different direction."

00:05:56.934 --> 00:05:58.767
Or, "Just get out."

00:05:58.767 --> 00:06:00.367
Like, you know?

00:06:00.367 --> 00:06:01.868
-Looking back,

00:06:01.868 --> 00:06:03.234
do you think in most
of those cases

00:06:03.234 --> 00:06:04.400
they were right to
send you home?

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:05.834
-Yes, absolutely.

00:06:05.834 --> 00:06:08.067
Oh, my God, one job,
I used to --

00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:10.734
I thought I was going
to be a receptionist.

00:06:10.734 --> 00:06:13.067
[ Laughing loudly ]

00:06:16.834 --> 00:06:18.701
It's hilarious, right?

00:06:18.701 --> 00:06:21.400
[ Laughs ] I couldn't
even answer the phone.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:22.801
I think it was
a construction company.

00:06:22.801 --> 00:06:24.200
I'd be like,
"Alpha Construction."

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:25.734
And the girl was like,
"You got to be more..."

00:06:25.734 --> 00:06:28.734
I was like,
"Alpha Construction."

00:06:28.734 --> 00:06:31.534
And they would come to --
Man, I would go to sleep.

00:06:31.534 --> 00:06:34.267
I would go to sleep at the desk.

00:06:34.267 --> 00:06:36.167
[ Laughter ]

00:06:36.167 --> 00:06:38.033
Because I did comedy
the night before,

00:06:38.033 --> 00:06:39.701
and I was like,
"Man, I'm finna take a nap."

00:06:39.701 --> 00:06:43.367
"You are the receptionist.
You can't take a nap."

00:06:43.367 --> 00:06:46.634
So I won a contest
and I came in the next day

00:06:46.634 --> 00:06:48.801
and I was bragging, like,
"I won this contest.

00:06:48.801 --> 00:06:50.534
I won $1,000 in this contest."

00:06:50.534 --> 00:06:51.801
And they was like, "Oh, great."

00:06:51.801 --> 00:06:52.968
And the girl called me
in the office.

00:06:52.968 --> 00:06:54.501
She was like, you know,
"I'm so glad

00:06:54.501 --> 00:06:58.367
that the comedy is going well...

00:06:58.367 --> 00:07:02.133
because we're going to have
to let you go."

00:07:02.133 --> 00:07:03.901
-When she heard
you won a contest,

00:07:03.901 --> 00:07:05.868
she was like,
"Today is the day."

00:07:05.868 --> 00:07:07.567
-Today is the perfect day.

00:07:07.567 --> 00:07:08.601
-The window opened a little bit.

00:07:08.601 --> 00:07:09.901
-Oh, I clowned, too.

00:07:09.901 --> 00:07:12.934
-I was like, "Oh, bitch,
I'm finna be famous!"

00:07:14.968 --> 00:07:18.634
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:18.634 --> 00:07:20.767
-You should go back
with all the money you have now.

00:07:20.767 --> 00:07:23.000
You should buy that desk
and use it to take naps on now.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:25.033
-They ain't open no more.
-They ain't open no more?

00:07:25.033 --> 00:07:27.834
Well, yeah. Nobody there
to answer the phones.

00:07:27.834 --> 00:07:29.067
Hey, this is very exciting.

00:07:29.067 --> 00:07:32.067
Next week,
you were the first guest host

00:07:32.067 --> 00:07:33.367
when "The Daily Show"
started doing this

00:07:33.367 --> 00:07:35.100
and you get to go back,
do a second week.

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:38.767
You were fantastic, fantastic
the first time.

00:07:38.767 --> 00:07:40.133
[ Cheering ]
-Yes.

00:07:40.133 --> 00:07:42.267
Are you excited? And my other,
just quick follow-up --

00:07:42.267 --> 00:07:44.267
do you feel like you understand
it a little bit more

00:07:44.267 --> 00:07:45.734
going into it now,
having done a week?

00:07:45.734 --> 00:07:50.133
-Oh first of all, it's so ran
like a well-oiled machine.

00:07:50.133 --> 00:07:52.434
-They know what they're doing.
-They'd make anybody look good.

00:07:52.434 --> 00:07:55.534
I'm going to be honest with you.
They make anybody look good.

00:07:55.534 --> 00:07:59.067
Their writers over there
are just like, big brains.

00:07:59.067 --> 00:08:00.934
They're just --

00:08:00.934 --> 00:08:03.033
They just, like,
some of the stuff?

00:08:03.033 --> 00:08:04.968
I love it.
I love it over there.

00:08:04.968 --> 00:08:06.901
And I love to
be able to express,

00:08:06.901 --> 00:08:09.634
because I feel like I'm just
like the people's people.

00:08:09.634 --> 00:08:11.167
I understand
what they understand.

00:08:11.167 --> 00:08:13.734
Like, I don't know
if you saw that

00:08:13.734 --> 00:08:15.701
that I thought that
the Electoral College

00:08:15.701 --> 00:08:17.601
was where the politicians
go to college.

00:08:17.601 --> 00:08:18.934
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:08:18.934 --> 00:08:21.100
-And they learn college stuff,
you know what I mean?

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:23.367
They learn, you know,
it's a whole class.

00:08:23.367 --> 00:08:25.200
So I was like,
how you gonna know

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:26.534
what the Constitution say?

00:08:26.534 --> 00:08:30.901
So, you know, I was explained
that that's not right.

00:08:30.901 --> 00:08:33.334
-Yeah.
-So it's like, stuff like that.

00:08:33.334 --> 00:08:37.667
It's a lot of stuff that
people are not informed about.

00:08:37.667 --> 00:08:40.634
And I feel like you
get to watch MSNBC

00:08:40.634 --> 00:08:42.267
and CNN and get all of that.

00:08:42.267 --> 00:08:44.133
Now come here so I can
make you laugh about it.

00:08:44.133 --> 00:08:48.033
That's what I want,
to make people laugh.

00:08:48.033 --> 00:08:51.868
You guys need to laugh, man.
Y'all not laughing.

00:08:51.868 --> 00:08:53.801
[ Applause ]
Y'all just so sad.

00:08:53.801 --> 00:08:57.133
Everybody's so sad right now.

00:08:57.133 --> 00:08:59.801
And it's just like,
we as comedians,

00:08:59.801 --> 00:09:02.467
me as a comedian
I feel a big debt

00:09:02.467 --> 00:09:06.067
to this world right now
to bring some joy.

00:09:06.067 --> 00:09:08.901
Like, it's not all hopeless,
I promise.

00:09:08.901 --> 00:09:10.000
[ Applause ]
And I know.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:12.934
Listen, listen,
I know that you're like,

00:09:12.934 --> 00:09:15.601
"Oh, bitch, you're rich,
and blah, blah, blah,

00:09:15.601 --> 00:09:17.467
and you can't tell me,
blah, blah, blah."

00:09:17.467 --> 00:09:19.801
Yes, I can.

00:09:19.801 --> 00:09:20.934
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:20.934 --> 00:09:22.701
Yes, I can.

00:09:22.701 --> 00:09:24.734
We have to have hope.

00:09:24.734 --> 00:09:26.834
You have to believe
in yourself

00:09:26.834 --> 00:09:28.968
and in the people that you --

00:09:28.968 --> 00:09:33.501
We have to work together
and we have to be uncomfortable.

00:09:33.501 --> 00:09:34.567
That is the problem.

00:09:34.567 --> 00:09:35.868
We're not doing anything

00:09:35.868 --> 00:09:38.734
because we're complacent
and we're comfortable.

00:09:38.734 --> 00:09:41.801
We have to be uncomfortable
to fix some of this [bleep].

00:09:41.801 --> 00:09:43.033
You know what I'm saying?

00:09:43.033 --> 00:09:45.934
[ Cheers and applause ]
Real talk.

00:09:45.934 --> 00:09:48.868
Get your ass up and vote.

00:09:48.868 --> 00:09:50.834
Only a third of the country
is voting.

00:09:50.834 --> 00:09:54.901
If 60 people are in the room

00:09:54.901 --> 00:09:59.334
and only 20 are ordering food,
guess what you gonna eat?

00:09:59.334 --> 00:10:02.367
What the 20 people ordered!

00:10:02.367 --> 00:10:05.467
Pick your [bleep] menu up!

00:10:05.467 --> 00:10:09.300
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:10:12.701 --> 00:10:16.567
-Well, I disagree
with everything she just said.

00:10:16.567 --> 00:10:18.033
Another really cool thing,

00:10:18.033 --> 00:10:19.601
before you go, everybody here
in the audience tonight --

00:10:19.601 --> 00:10:20.734
talking about bringing joy --

00:10:20.734 --> 00:10:22.334
is going to get a copy
of Leslie's book.

00:10:22.334 --> 00:10:24.834
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Yes!

00:10:24.834 --> 00:10:26.701
-We'll be right back
with Judd Apatow.

00:10:26.701 --> 00:10:27.801
There's nobody like her!

